      "created_at": "2026-03-15T17:52:01Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-03-15T17:52:01Z",
      "body": "> I think this is due to hmac_digest using the implicit default_context_int libctx inside openssl, which doesn't seem thread safe on startup\n\nOpenSSL uses pthread_once in this code path. It looks thread-safe to me.\n\nThe only crash I've been able to reproduce is a crash **at exit**. OpenSSL 3.0.19 includes an atexit handler that `OPENSSL_cleanup` that frees internal locks and will give you a crash with the same stack trace as you reported.\n\nHere's a repro of the atexit crash:\n\n```python\nimport threading, hmac\nbig = b\"x\" * 100_000_000\ndef w():\n    while True:\n        hmac.digest(b\"key\", big, \"sha256\")\nfor i in range(4):\n    threading.Thread(target=w, daemon=True).start()\n```\n\n* This happens with `hmac.digest(...)` but not `hmac.new(...).digest()` because the `Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS` call happens in different places in the two code paths.\n* It happens with OpenSSL 3.0.19 but not 3.5.4\n* It happens with both free-threading and the GIL-enabled build\n\nSee also:\n* https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/114653#issuecomment-1925893837\n* https://github.com/pyca/cryptography/issues/10368",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
